title: Seventy-six birthdays
from: Matthias Oduya
gist: The Counsellor's small talk on why he knows everyone's birthday, and why you might learn one too.
== lines
oduya: Good evening. Come in, take your time. The sweets bowl is going round. Green ones too.   [gesture]
oduya: I know the birthday of everyone aboard. Seventy-six, counting you. People find that odd.   [laugh]
oduya: Here's why. A long way from home, a birthday is the day people notice you.
oduya: So I make sure somebody does. A card under the door, a song in the mess, a word.
oduya: You don't need my list. Ask someone when theirs is. Then remember. That's all it takes.
oduya: It's a small thing. Most good things on a ship are. I'll stop there and let you ask.   [nod]
? choice
* Who has the next birthday?   rel:oduya+2 flavour:cheer mem:talk.oduya.asked
  oduya: Ah. That would spoil a card. Keep your ears open in the mess this week.   [laugh]
* Who remembers yours?   rel:oduya+2 flavour:listen mem:talk.oduya.asked
  oduya: Chef Brennan. Every year, a cake I didn't ask for. It's the best part of the day.   [nod]
* (Just listen)
  oduya: No questions. That's all right. Quiet is good too.
oduya: Thank you for coming. Have a sweet as you go. Not the last green one.
